# The thread dump of debug and test builds (roadmap l4.md L2), on the AT's
# Sys Req key: every thread, its state and priority, and how much of its
# kernel stack it has used. The keyboard driver asks for the dump (roadmap
# l4.md L8), and is running; the shell waits for the reply of the OS
# server's thread, which looks for a key once a tick; the OS server's
# control thread awaits Ctrl+Break; the mouse's driver is still in the
# mouse's reset (roadmap l4.md L7). Whether the shell's OS thread is in its
# tick's sleep or just out of it depends on the emulator's timing.
program: shell
keys: {sysrq}
match: contains
---
rx286: thread root receiving priority 7, kernel stack used
rx286: thread printer receiving priority 6, kernel stack used
rx286: thread mouse awaiting a notification priority 6, kernel stack used
rx286: thread console receiving priority 6, kernel stack used
rx286: thread kbd running priority 6, kernel stack used
rx286: thread program awaiting its reply priority 3, kernel stack used
rx286: thread counter ready priority 1, kernel stack used
rx286: thread os awaiting a notification priority 5, kernel stack used
@bochs rx286: thread os ready priority 5, kernel stack used
@mame rx286: thread os ready priority 5, kernel stack used
@dosbox-x rx286: thread os waiting priority 5, kernel stack used
rx286: thread os receiving priority 5, kernel stack used
